\section*{7.1 Herbs, Shrubs and Trees}

Look at the plants around you. Some plants are small with soft, green stems.
They are called herbs. Mint and coriander are herbs. Some plants have hard
stems that branch near the base. They are called shrubs. Rose and hibiscus
are shrubs. Tall plants with hard, thick, woody stems are called trees. The
mango tree and the neem tree are familiar examples.

\section*{Activity 1}

Collect five different leaves on your way to school. Place each leaf under a
sheet of paper and rub a pencil over the paper. Compare the patterns you see.
What do the lines on each leaf look like?

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/acceptance-cbse6-sci-01.jpg?height=420&width=640&top_left_y=250&top_left_x=120}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 7.1 - Leaf impressions showing different vein patterns.}
\end{figure}

The lines on a leaf are called veins. The pattern of veins is the venation of
the leaf. When the veins form a net on both sides of the midrib, the venation
is reticulate. When the veins run parallel to one another, the venation is
parallel.

\section*{Do you know?}

The banyan tree grows special roots from its branches. These hanging roots
reach the ground and act like extra trunks. One banyan tree in a botanical
garden has more than three thousand such prop roots.

\section*{7.2 The Stem Carries Water}

The stem carries water from the roots to the leaves.

